#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157068 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/id #uuid "e5b64c26-300d-530d-b22f-ff061f4e3296" :question/math-academy-id "e-7657" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4e^x - 5 >  0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{x}-5 & >0 \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & >5 \\\\ {e}^{x} & >\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ x & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157069 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "95104c90-f452-5551-adec-836ef1192276" :question/math-academy-id "e-7660" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3 \\cdot 5^x <   7^{3-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {5}^{x} & <{7}^{3-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {5}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(3-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7) & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157075 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/id #uuid "1c336514-aac7-5f9c-8118-8a16188f2d42" :question/math-academy-id "e-7703" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5 \\cdot \\left( \\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^x + 2 \\geq 12.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}+2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 10 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\ln 3 - \\ln 4$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157076 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/id #uuid "185e4c00-f218-5d54-8345-397267a74b6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7704" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4 \\cdot 3^{8-x} + 12 > 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {3}^{8-x}+12 & >0 \\\\ 4\\cdot {3}^{8-x} & >-12 \\\\ {3}^{8-x} & >-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n3^{8-x} > 0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality $3^{8-x} > -3$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0725bf8b-0c78-536f-b1d3-1a261598213e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+e)+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282199} {:answer/id #uuid "fca80cb4-d33a-5f30-bb05-4bb18d2f9db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282200} {:answer/id #uuid "38c29295-5a2c-52b3-b869-7266b153c7fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<2-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282201} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8e74f8-a88a-52eb-b519-4a3b1c3bc46d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282202} {:answer/id #uuid "4d80cec8-9598-53c3-b8b6-18a55c1eb8b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+e)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fca80cb4-d33a-5f30-bb05-4bb18d2f9db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282200} :answer-field/id #uuid "f623cf69-21d6-59b6-bfeb-2652236d2396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5ddbd9f-8150-5833-819a-e65c26b2e497" :question/math-academy-id "q-115205" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2{e}^{x-2}+1<3.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x-2}+1 & <3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x-2} & <2 \\\\ {e}^{x-2} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-2}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\ x-2 & <0 \\\\ x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31524f6f-9455-5698-839b-7c8011f5f2fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282206} {:answer/id #uuid "3101d6b6-76c6-5bfd-9ca7-5e4ce12c4c33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282207} {:answer/id #uuid "d46004f7-34ec-5bd0-b351-1705c4d16eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282208} {:answer/id #uuid "b8deb015-650a-5e4b-82bf-eca48b4c62e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282209} {:answer/id #uuid "5988bfda-7332-516f-8edd-453ba7812201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d46004f7-34ec-5bd0-b351-1705c4d16eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282208} :answer-field/id #uuid "e11fd5a1-6666-52f1-95ec-ed1f9256af9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f78e66ee-bef1-5f44-a0f0-8d3c8b5e36fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-115212" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2{(0.7)}^{x+9}\\ge 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(0.7)}^{x+9} & \\ge 4 \\\\ {(0.7)}^{x+9} & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.7)^{x+9}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ (x+9)\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7 & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+9 & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7} \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}-9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1f78af6-c7ed-5d65-a74b-7108b8a9aa16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282213} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c2e384-c1a0-5f8b-9458-7ddb2be0f3fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282214} {:answer/id #uuid "4bae2ce8-d17c-5660-8dd4-c21070826cb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282215} {:answer/id #uuid "41e303d1-33ee-53bf-90d9-c30bb87fe7d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282216} {:answer/id #uuid "b1352605-6ad5-5065-bec5-692305ff4b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1352605-6ad5-5065-bec5-692305ff4b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282217} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb532446-b49d-5fdb-9992-a86be9d0391b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53c92145-52c7-5881-9ab5-7c588ac0f4ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-115222" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x+6}+1\\ge 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x+6}+1 & \\ge 2 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x+6} & \\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x+6}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\ (x+6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+6)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+6)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+6) & \\le \\frac{0}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ x+6 & \\le 0 \\\\ x & \\le -6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f84df997-3018-5d91-8aee-58aa2c46f7d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282220} {:answer/id #uuid "53e86893-596b-51f4-a5e4-08f245fe9437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282221} {:answer/id #uuid "c14fb307-133d-52b2-b4bc-1d999f964c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 7-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282222} {:answer/id #uuid "b4422e9c-af0a-5de6-8fb5-b47d868ce212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282223} {:answer/id #uuid "12445463-8ce5-5fd5-980b-01ed76d691f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{12\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.72+7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4422e9c-af0a-5de6-8fb5-b47d868ce212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282223} :answer-field/id #uuid "27310d58-d1ca-5fd4-aaa6-8b3134afc3ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cea960fb-2ca9-5542-b255-685beb6fda52" :question/math-academy-id "q-115230" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{2}(0.72)^{3x-7}+2\\le -4.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(0.72)^{3x-7}+2 & \\le -4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0.72)^{3x-7} & \\le -6 \\\\ (0.72)^{3x-7} & \\le -12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n(0.72)^{3x-7}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality $(0.72)^{3x-7}\\le -12$ has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2189878f-c4df-5273-b708-8a810979fd7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282227} {:answer/id #uuid "ce87e08c-8aea-5c2b-b14b-14246457819c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282228} {:answer/id #uuid "2e26e4b5-6a71-5d1a-a923-09e99de73132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282229} {:answer/id #uuid "9965673c-bddd-540e-a95f-819e8af20098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282230} {:answer/id #uuid "fba4583f-0c0f-5123-93d3-0f7201d59a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce87e08c-8aea-5c2b-b14b-14246457819c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282228} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de3d14d-5d9d-52a4-b53d-41eca44b1665" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad267c36-a064-5e17-8942-0415d6eebd12" :question/math-academy-id "q-115231" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5}+4>-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5}+4 & >-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5} & >-6 \\\\ (0.43)^{4x+5} & >-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n(0.43)^{4x+5}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality $(0.43)^{4x+5}>-4$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b674cc54-7a52-594f-aeb7-7c11ef13910b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282234} {:answer/id #uuid "2986a2a6-1141-51b5-9c60-5909ec42811e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282235} {:answer/id #uuid "626ad8ab-087f-5619-ac82-1e6b8014ed00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282236} {:answer/id #uuid "1c2891c1-a9c3-58c8-85bf-4d857f2d35cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282237} {:answer/id #uuid "850b94c3-1d43-5571-852b-a4b1fb129952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b674cc54-7a52-594f-aeb7-7c11ef13910b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282234} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1203ce1-d167-5ef4-9ac8-8ec756aa5a3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7904da67-2a80-5fe6-93ea-370ad7d9b2da" :question/math-academy-id "q-115248" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${7}^{x+1}>{5}^{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{x+1} & >{5}^{x-1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{x+1}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x-1}) \\\\ (x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & >(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & >x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x & >-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ffae442-b149-5e65-af75-d60e565e419e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282241} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb88958-b780-539d-b197-f48c81ba1417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282242} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4e3aa0-abe9-5342-a2be-5a14389ded1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282243} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2a63ed-a882-52e7-aa8a-6e2b33dd9486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282244} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed8e0aa-7313-5487-bac9-d9ab578e6fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb2a63ed-a882-52e7-aa8a-6e2b33dd9486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282244} :answer-field/id #uuid "95526973-c5d1-5eb0-99aa-839a08d4d898" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46f93566-72fe-564d-b0b7-ec11d50de229" :question/math-academy-id "q-115250" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${5}^{x-1}<{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x-1} & <{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x-1}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2-x}] \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28173a38-6dc9-5fd2-a61c-f52b4f6e70e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28173a38-6dc9-5fd2-a61c-f52b4f6e70e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282248} :answer-field/id #uuid "8db2f32b-274d-5e59-a578-1054bbe672fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282247} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2459edc8-1840-550d-919b-e265dd3af5bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln(24)}{2\\ln(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2459edc8-1840-550d-919b-e265dd3af5bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln(24)}{2\\ln(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282250} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa7f84d-4a73-563e-b878-79f01f074242" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd62bfd9-6b42-572a-8c38-43a6755aee95" :question/math-academy-id "q-277844" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x-1}\\ge 2.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x-1} & \\ge 2 \\\\ {3}^{2x-1} & \\ge 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $3$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{2x-1}) & \\ge {log}_{3}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x-1 & \\ge {log}_{3}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x & \\ge {log}_{3}⁡(8)+1 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{1}{2}({log}_{3}⁡(8)+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb1fa160-55f8-5a70-91a9-35c92680ac06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282253} {:answer/id #uuid "78682677-fcab-5274-af12-4b1dcd9d5281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb1fa160-55f8-5a70-91a9-35c92680ac06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282253} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7e11152-bd30-5485-9804-f461f5a0895e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282252} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11f1f561-10ad-59a2-b058-10363ae0d32c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln(14)}{\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11f1f561-10ad-59a2-b058-10363ae0d32c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln(14)}{\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282256} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b1ad023-ebec-5f19-95cc-47e1b6b7e605" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8711faf-ad47-5c1d-b8a5-bf5e50a40321" :question/math-academy-id "q-277846" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{x}>7.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{x} & >7 \\\\ {4}^{x} & >14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $4$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({4}^{x}) & >{log}_{4}⁡(14) \\\\ x & >{log}_{4}⁡(14).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11247a3f-26f3-5730-b560-14e9bc3fec26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11247a3f-26f3-5730-b560-14e9bc3fec26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282259} :answer-field/id #uuid "16f353a0-630f-5937-ba75-6adb97e42f0e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282258} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d1417a4-eea8-575c-84d4-7a320c1b45c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\ln(12)}{\\ln(9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d1417a4-eea8-575c-84d4-7a320c1b45c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\ln(12)}{\\ln(9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282261} :answer-field/id #uuid "351a3c4f-018c-57d0-8db2-1034d851cdd5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b74d80da-84b6-5b4d-af66-de07a7526db7" :question/math-academy-id "q-277847" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $8\\cdot {9}^{x}\\ge \\frac{2}{3}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot {9}^{x} & \\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {9}^{x} & \\ge \\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $9$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}⁡({9}^{x}) & \\ge {log}_{9}⁡(\\frac{1}{12}) \\\\ {log}_{9}⁡({9}^{x}) & \\ge {log}_{9}⁡({12}^{-1}) \\\\ x & \\ge -{log}_{9}⁡(12).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58084965-2217-5fb7-a5a8-ece0b61b6082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58084965-2217-5fb7-a5a8-ece0b61b6082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282264} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5a8692b-8a83-5a1d-950f-d8d7dae08f9c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282263} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67391286-5a5f-51c4-a511-707250724944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\ln(3)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67391286-5a5f-51c4-a511-707250724944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\ln(3)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282266} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8bf2fbc-0be1-5b22-938b-6ca26cf79bac" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2083d32e-7721-5866-8074-0c239f215fa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-277853" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{3{e}^{2-3x}}{\\sqrt{3}}<-2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{3{e}^{2-3x}}{\\sqrt{3}} & <-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3{e}^{2-3x}}{\\sqrt{3}} & <-3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3{e}^{2-3x} & <-3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3{e}^{2-3x} & <-9 \\\\ {e}^{2-3x} & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-3x}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 2-3x & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ -3x & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2 \\\\ 3x & <2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{1}{3}(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd8e4cac-b7be-57e1-9847-86677d582263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.54)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2e)}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282269} {:answer/id #uuid "189672dd-70cb-541b-b857-64ffc4c2edb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.54)}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282270} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e983e0-872c-542b-b9fe-144d34374c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.54)}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282271} {:answer/id #uuid "068e84d9-6429-5ecc-9038-e51b9a1cc340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.54)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4e)}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282272} {:answer/id #uuid "561c520d-a76c-579a-b020-79b30a676e0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.54)}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3e983e0-872c-542b-b9fe-144d34374c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.54)}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282271} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ebcf297-43e8-5786-9584-413e92833c2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2927fe24-07db-5e34-9585-ee2e3af94b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-365308" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{4}{(0.54)}^{x+3}-2e\\ge e.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{(0.54)}^{x+3}-2e & \\ge e \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}{(0.54)}^{x+3} & \\ge 3e \\\\ {(0.54)}^{x+3} & \\ge 4e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.54)^{x+3}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(4e) \\\\ (x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.54) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(4e)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.54)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.54)} \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.54)}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3ac57a6-e60d-5218-8794-04417dec905e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282276} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb8a6ae-50ec-5fd0-ae5a-fcd2a175dd9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282277} {:answer/id #uuid "67cac48b-8c71-5763-b914-eb64c911cfee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282278} {:answer/id #uuid "5b34a53f-358e-5a70-a28e-6565ed5751bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282279} {:answer/id #uuid "d61f7be1-563e-5776-a6bb-c21238a8bc31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eb8a6ae-50ec-5fd0-ae5a-fcd2a175dd9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282277} :answer-field/id #uuid "73e9ac04-cbcb-5c74-b132-98f3daa7a52b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9916c25-ee44-597a-a249-18ef40c09255" :question/math-academy-id "q-365323" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${4}^{1-2x}\\le 3\\cdot {5}^{2-3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{1-2x} & \\le 3\\cdot {5}^{2-3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{1-2x}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {5}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{1-2x}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{2-3x}) \\\\ (1-2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡4-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90913424-565e-5da9-bd57-a11fdd7c7d49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282283} {:answer/id #uuid "14c86740-0847-5b12-91fe-76036ea3f978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282284} {:answer/id #uuid "b46366c1-5d12-533b-8af9-b76ae66085f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282285} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f6ddce-8b06-5403-9913-49c89bcb251c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282286} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e67c13-1127-5d57-883e-d4ebcb4afc3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3e67c13-1127-5d57-883e-d4ebcb4afc3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282287} :answer-field/id #uuid "216bc7a5-eba3-5593-adf4-e104f5f0d58f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db98e2be-d4e2-5d82-8a5f-f833ad907bed" :question/math-academy-id "q-365326" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}>{3}^{2-3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2} & >{3}^{2-3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡[3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}] & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}] & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(2-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a7d1d1a-ed8a-5886-bde3-d2c482abe3d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282290} {:answer/id #uuid "f2116fb8-7bb1-5de7-b7ae-dedf7e9021c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282291} {:answer/id #uuid "5bce91c7-2378-5550-b238-6fda7740fe7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282292} {:answer/id #uuid "aa234ef4-ee3c-57db-82f5-c1d69e769d21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282293} {:answer/id #uuid "e70c3a62-ead6-5533-bec8-ab8aaba6337b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a7d1d1a-ed8a-5886-bde3-d2c482abe3d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282290} :answer-field/id #uuid "be109be0-7d81-59f9-8107-12d4523a5e57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "078bdf1c-b6d1-5ab4-9023-b94588024657" :question/math-academy-id "q-87828" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x}<25.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x} & <25 \\\\ {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x} & <5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x}] & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{9}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "661dcbf8-3a68-5651-bff8-a48afc11bad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282297} {:answer/id #uuid "98c6be13-ee07-569e-b47a-a3b109110d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282298} {:answer/id #uuid "0feb2b7c-3f51-55cb-ac1a-97475cc2519b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282299} {:answer/id #uuid "7941a20d-a88d-520e-bc10-20960fa2afbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282300} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbe2f79-a385-5aff-a677-4ae13f499f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0feb2b7c-3f51-55cb-ac1a-97475cc2519b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282299} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8cd88b8-3264-561f-b984-78b8dbc1ea35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ead7b70a-ed06-52b4-b721-a1de1a689310" :question/math-academy-id "q-87833" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${(0.84)}^{x+5}-20>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(0.84)}^{x+5}-20 & >0 \\\\ {(0.84)}^{x+5} & >20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.84)^{x+5}] & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20) \\\\ (x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)} \\\\ x & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e101ace2-e87c-5a2c-ad48-90ccf9081e9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282304} {:answer/id #uuid "cf14c630-59ec-500b-a6e9-40ff8c22a4bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282305} {:answer/id #uuid "05b54df9-9317-53e0-b380-658da7c278db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282306} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f0894a-beb1-5ff9-8980-78d2cef5b68f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282307} {:answer/id #uuid "deeeae5c-3db4-5076-a088-17f188e3f16f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05b54df9-9317-53e0-b380-658da7c278db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282306} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4bebf12-c808-5950-ab72-aa79531416ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f40cc1d-d1d7-51d2-a488-552b848ee0e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-87872" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4-{e}^{x+1}>6.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-{e}^{x+1} & >6 \\\\ -{e}^{x+1} & >2 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & <-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{e}^{x+1}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality ${e}^{x+1}<-2$ has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc522e96-0680-5b25-9401-2424de5b3f0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282311} {:answer/id #uuid "79e0c68f-8a4a-52ea-8966-a053026fe909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282312} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2c2635-1d57-5d0d-a439-fa518ff831ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282313} {:answer/id #uuid "e50992ad-a7ea-5b22-9289-c1f38493f8a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282314} {:answer/id #uuid "b342574a-db9d-5bed-b7c2-cbacd73be65e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a2c2635-1d57-5d0d-a439-fa518ff831ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282313} :answer-field/id #uuid "a49c9fb6-1c7d-5ef5-9a9f-bcbafd6d918d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "defdb5da-77d3-5e00-9ea9-cb01ec730662" :question/math-academy-id "q-87874" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{5}-{e}^{x-2}>\\frac{4}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}-{e}^{x-2} & >\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -{e}^{x-2} & >\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ {e}^{x-2} & <-\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{e}^{x-2}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality ${e}^{x-2}<-\\frac{3}{5}$ has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f7bcf35-010a-5bff-8aa2-0361632812dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282318} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdea6a6-0c79-531a-96da-b07474b8bf39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<3-{log}_{3}⁡(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282319} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd039f6-e137-5dd7-bff5-566777db2431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<5-{log}_{6}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282320} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2dac14-e6bb-53b7-aa1b-c45b57ea410e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282321} {:answer/id #uuid "58687b13-53da-511f-bd8d-ffd218b8fe74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>6-{log}_{5}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f7bcf35-010a-5bff-8aa2-0361632812dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282318} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d76992-82a4-5338-b5ac-86f947fdd1cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5f64ef7-fc02-5b6d-a8c5-440eac71e226" :question/math-academy-id "q-87893" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x}+6>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x}+6 & >0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x} & >-6 \\\\ {3}^{1-5x} & >-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{3}^{1-5x}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality ${3}^{1-5x}>-10$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40cf9b82-335c-512d-a0bc-c58fa62767dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282325} {:answer/id #uuid "2166ca04-f550-589b-8b8c-a4949ed6cfec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282326} {:answer/id #uuid "d320d51a-9bd0-55e1-909b-4d02bb7de032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282327} {:answer/id #uuid "3919d247-e231-5b4f-89be-fa07c201e901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282328} {:answer/id #uuid "822f19e6-2aab-5194-ae5c-99b1f63079fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "822f19e6-2aab-5194-ae5c-99b1f63079fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282329} :answer-field/id #uuid "aee18e45-d093-534a-a7d7-d43c16bfeecd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40d4bda5-69c1-5c56-8e06-e9265d3522f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-87908" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5\\cdot {2}^{x}<{3}^{2-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {2}^{x} & <{3}^{2-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(5\\cdot {2}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡5+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & <(2-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡5+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & <2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & <2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & <2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x & <\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
